1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,180
When you ask an AI chatbot a simple

2
00:00:03,180 --> 00:00:06,260
question, the electricity behind the answer is tiny.

3
00:00:06,210 --> 00:00:09,521
Google measured its typical Gemini text prompt in

4
00:00:09,521 --> 00:00:12,832
2025 at about a quarter of a watt-hour.

5
00:00:12,832 --> 00:00:15,173
Google says that's about the same

6
00:00:15,173 --> 00:00:17,513
as watching nine seconds of TV.

7
00:00:18,018 --> 00:00:20,641
So here's what doesn't add up.

8
00:00:20,591 --> 00:00:25,305
In 2025, the electricity used by data centers worldwide grew

9
00:00:25,305 --> 00:00:30,019
by almost twenty percent, according to the International Energy Agency.

10
00:00:30,536 --> 00:00:34,534
And according to the IEA, just five tech companies now spend

11
00:00:34,534 --> 00:00:38,532
more on construction and equipment, mostly for data centers, than the

12
00:00:38,532 --> 00:00:41,802
entire world invests in producing oil and natural gas.

13
00:00:42,262 --> 00:00:44,473
Tiny per question.

14
00:00:44,423 --> 00:00:46,670
Enormous in total.

15
00:00:46,670 --> 00:00:49,799
Today we're going to follow the electricity, from the

16
00:00:49,799 --> 00:00:52,929
high-voltage line outside, through the building, into a single

17
00:00:52,929 --> 00:00:56,058
cabinet of AI chips, and out the other side.

18
00:00:56,580 --> 00:00:59,738
Along the way I'll show you the one number engineers

19
00:00:59,738 --> 00:01:02,581
use to tell whether a data center is wasteful.

20
00:01:02,531 --> 00:01:06,108
And we'll find out where almost every watt ends up.

21
00:01:06,108 --> 00:01:08,714
Once you see that, the whole AI

22
00:01:08,714 --> 00:01:10,948
power story starts to make sense.

23
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:14,781
Let's start with scale, because these numbers get thrown

24
00:01:14,781 --> 00:01:17,661
around a lot, and they're easy to mix up.

25
00:01:18,130 --> 00:01:22,468
Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory, in a report for the

26
00:01:22,468 --> 00:01:26,807
Department of Energy, estimated that U.S. data centers used

27
00:01:26,807 --> 00:01:30,181
about 176 terawatt-hours of electricity in 2023.

28
00:01:30,131 --> 00:01:32,631
That was 4.4 percent of all

29
00:01:32,631 --> 00:01:35,131
the electricity used in the country.

30
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:40,617
A terawatt-hour is a billion kilowatt-hours, so let's bring that home.

31
00:01:40,567 --> 00:01:45,281
The average American household used about 860 kilowatt-hours a

32
00:01:45,281 --> 00:01:49,995
month in 2023, according to the Energy Information Administration.

33
00:01:50,510 --> 00:01:54,255
Run the math, and U.S. data centers used about

34
00:01:54,255 --> 00:01:58,000
as much electricity in 2023 as seventeen million homes.

35
00:01:58,468 --> 00:02:00,426
And it's climbing.

36
00:02:00,376 --> 00:02:04,731
The same Berkeley Lab report projects data centers could use

37
00:02:04,731 --> 00:02:09,086
between 6.7 and 12 percent of U.S. electricity by 2028.

38
00:02:09,086 --> 00:02:12,690
That's a wide range, and it's wide on purpose.

39
00:02:12,690 --> 00:02:16,427
Nobody knows exactly how fast AI will spread.

40
00:02:16,938 --> 00:02:22,157
Worldwide, the IEA put data centers at about 415 terawatt-hours in

41
00:02:22,157 --> 00:02:26,901
2024, roughly one and a half percent of global electricity.

42
00:02:26,851 --> 00:02:31,319
The United States alone accounted for 45 percent of that.

43
00:02:31,832 --> 00:02:35,386
Individual sites are getting huge, too.

44
00:02:35,336 --> 00:02:39,011
The IEA says a typical AI-focused data center uses

45
00:02:39,011 --> 00:02:42,277
as much electricity as a hundred thousand households.

46
00:02:42,277 --> 00:02:46,586
The biggest ones under construction will use twenty times that.

47
00:02:47,536 --> 00:02:50,810
So how does all that power actually get in?

48
00:02:50,760 --> 00:02:54,257
Usually on high-voltage transmission lines, running at tens

49
00:02:54,257 --> 00:02:57,316
or even hundreds of thousands of volts.

50
00:02:57,822 --> 00:03:01,102
At a substation, often built right next to

51
00:03:01,102 --> 00:03:04,381
the campus, big transformers step that voltage down.

52
00:03:04,331 --> 00:03:07,755
Then it gets stepped down again, and again, until it

53
00:03:07,755 --> 00:03:11,179
reaches the few hundred volts the equipment inside can handle.

54
00:03:11,684 --> 00:03:15,662
Before it reaches a single chip, the power runs through an

55
00:03:15,662 --> 00:03:19,639
uninterruptible power supply, which is basically a room full of batteries.

56
00:03:20,098 --> 00:03:23,903
If the grid flickers, those batteries carry the whole building for

57
00:03:23,903 --> 00:03:27,708
the seconds or minutes it takes the backup generators to start.

58
00:03:28,176 --> 00:03:30,631
Those generators usually run on diesel

59
00:03:30,631 --> 00:03:33,086
and sit outside in long rows.

60
00:03:33,036 --> 00:03:36,773
Most of the year they do nothing except get tested.

61
00:03:36,773 --> 00:03:39,856
Their whole job is to make sure a thunderstorm

62
00:03:39,856 --> 00:03:42,597
two counties over never takes your email offline.

63
00:03:43,118 --> 00:03:46,805
This chain matters for a reason that's easy to miss.

64
00:03:46,755 --> 00:03:49,122
Every link needs heavy equipment, and a

65
00:03:49,122 --> 00:03:51,489
lot of it is in short supply.

66
00:03:51,489 --> 00:03:56,790
In 2026, the IEA listed gas turbines, transformers, grid connections and

67
00:03:56,790 --> 00:04:01,608
new transmission lines among the bottlenecks slowing data center construction.

68
00:04:02,566 --> 00:04:06,838
Now we're inside, walking down a long aisle of server racks.

69
00:04:06,788 --> 00:04:10,352
A rack is a tall metal cabinet, a bit bigger

70
00:04:10,352 --> 00:04:13,915
than a refrigerator, stacked with computers from floor to top.

71
00:04:14,436 --> 00:04:18,827
For years, a typical rack didn't need all that much power.

72
00:04:18,777 --> 00:04:22,402
Uptime Institute's 2024 global survey found the average

73
00:04:22,402 --> 00:04:25,573
rack still drew less than eight kilowatts.

74
00:04:25,573 --> 00:04:29,137
That's about five space heaters running on high.

75
00:04:29,642 --> 00:04:32,704
An AI rack is a different animal.

76
00:04:32,654 --> 00:04:38,206
Nvidia's GB200 NVL72 packs seventy-two graphics processors into one rack,

77
00:04:38,206 --> 00:04:43,757
and Nvidia's own documentation puts it at roughly 120 kilowatts.

78
00:04:44,272 --> 00:04:47,347
That's about fifteen times the average.

79
00:04:47,297 --> 00:04:49,788
If one of those racks ran flat out

80
00:04:49,788 --> 00:04:52,279
for a full year, it would use about

81
00:04:52,279 --> 00:04:54,770
as much electricity as a hundred American homes.

82
00:04:54,770 --> 00:04:56,792
One cabinet.

83
00:04:57,294 --> 00:05:00,104
And the biggest AI clusters link a hundred

84
00:05:00,104 --> 00:05:02,563
thousand or more of these chips together.

85
00:05:02,513 --> 00:05:05,685
At seventy-two per rack, that's well over a thousand

86
00:05:05,685 --> 00:05:08,856
of those cabinets, all humming in the same place.

87
00:05:09,356 --> 00:05:11,354
Why so hungry?

88
00:05:11,304 --> 00:05:15,626
An AI model is basically a gigantic pile of numbers.

89
00:05:15,626 --> 00:05:19,914
Training it, and answering every question afterward, means multiplying those

90
00:05:19,914 --> 00:05:24,203
numbers together over and over, trillions of times every second.

91
00:05:24,706 --> 00:05:26,962
Training a big model can keep

92
00:05:26,962 --> 00:05:29,217
thousands of chips busy for weeks.

93
00:05:29,167 --> 00:05:32,074
And once it's done, every single question

94
00:05:32,074 --> 00:05:34,566
anyone asks runs the model again.

95
00:05:35,088 --> 00:05:38,652
Graphics processors are built to do exactly that kind

96
00:05:38,652 --> 00:05:41,820
of math, thousands of calculations side by side.

97
00:05:41,770 --> 00:05:44,635
But cramming that much computing into one cabinet

98
00:05:44,635 --> 00:05:47,142
means cramming in a lot of power.

99
00:05:48,104 --> 00:05:51,073
So far we've followed the power in.

100
00:05:51,023 --> 00:05:54,071
Now comes the part almost nobody thinks about: what

101
00:05:54,071 --> 00:05:56,781
happens to it after the chip is done.

102
00:05:56,781 --> 00:05:59,267
This is where some data centers waste

103
00:05:59,267 --> 00:06:01,754
a lot, and others waste almost nothing.

104
00:06:02,278 --> 00:06:05,473
Here's the answer I promised at the start.

105
00:06:05,423 --> 00:06:09,731
Nearly all the electricity a chip uses turns into heat.

106
00:06:09,731 --> 00:06:12,790
A computer doesn't store energy.

107
00:06:12,790 --> 00:06:16,606
It does the math, and the energy leaves as warmth.

108
00:06:17,124 --> 00:06:20,676
So a 120-kilowatt rack is, in a

109
00:06:20,676 --> 00:06:24,228
very real sense, also a 120-kilowatt heater.

110
00:06:24,178 --> 00:06:27,058
And that heat has to come out constantly, or

111
00:06:27,058 --> 00:06:29,617
the chips slow themselves down or shut off.

112
00:06:30,122 --> 00:06:33,862
Older data centers handle this mostly with air.

113
00:06:33,812 --> 00:06:37,356
Cold air gets pushed into the aisles, server fans pull it

114
00:06:37,356 --> 00:06:40,900
through, and the hot air is carried off to cooling units.

115
00:06:41,416 --> 00:06:45,462
At AI densities, air alone struggles to keep up.

116
00:06:45,412 --> 00:06:48,020
So in Nvidia's design, liquid coolant flows through

117
00:06:48,020 --> 00:06:50,629
metal cold plates sitting right on the chips

118
00:06:50,629 --> 00:06:52,912
and carries the heat out through pipes.

119
00:06:53,430 --> 00:06:57,210
Getting rid of that heat often uses water as well.

120
00:06:57,160 --> 00:07:01,884
Berkeley Lab estimated U.S. data centers directly consumed about seventeen

121
00:07:01,884 --> 00:07:06,136
billion gallons of water in 2023, mostly for cooling.

122
00:07:06,644 --> 00:07:09,107
Now, the one number.

123
00:07:09,057 --> 00:07:13,419
It's called PUE, short for power usage effectiveness.

124
00:07:13,419 --> 00:07:16,729
Take all the power coming into the building and divide

125
00:07:16,729 --> 00:07:19,709
it by the power that actually reaches the computers.

126
00:07:20,218 --> 00:07:25,314
A perfect score is 1.0, meaning every watt goes to computing.

127
00:07:25,264 --> 00:07:29,660
Uptime Institute's 2025 survey put the global average at 1.54,

128
00:07:29,660 --> 00:07:33,615
and it has barely moved for six years running.

129
00:07:34,128 --> 00:07:37,259
In plain English, for every watt the servers use,

130
00:07:37,259 --> 00:07:40,390
the average data center spends about another half a

131
00:07:40,390 --> 00:07:43,174
watt on cooling, power conversion and everything else.

132
00:07:43,646 --> 00:07:46,827
The biggest operators do far better.

133
00:07:46,777 --> 00:07:52,442
Google reports a fleet-wide PUE of 1.09 for 2025.

134
00:07:52,442 --> 00:07:56,033
And the IEA says cooling ranges from about seven percent

135
00:07:56,033 --> 00:07:59,623
of a site's power in efficient hyperscale data centers to

136
00:07:59,623 --> 00:08:02,855
over thirty percent in older, less efficient enterprise ones.

137
00:08:03,814 --> 00:08:07,431
So if the chips keep getting more efficient, and the best

138
00:08:07,431 --> 00:08:10,719
buildings are already this good, why does demand keep climbing?

139
00:08:11,172 --> 00:08:14,446
Efficiency really is improving fast.

140
00:08:14,396 --> 00:08:18,825
Google says the energy behind its typical Gemini text

141
00:08:18,825 --> 00:08:23,253
prompt fell thirty-three-fold between May 2024 and May 2025.

142
00:08:23,762 --> 00:08:28,021
But cheaper answers invite more questions, and bigger jobs.

143
00:08:27,971 --> 00:08:30,983
The IEA's 2026 analysis says future growth depends on

144
00:08:30,983 --> 00:08:33,996
newer uses, like AI agents that grind through long

145
00:08:33,996 --> 00:08:37,008
tasks on their own and can use far more

146
00:08:37,008 --> 00:08:39,686
energy per request than a quick text answer.

147
00:08:40,192 --> 00:08:43,799
You can see that tension in Google's own numbers.

148
00:08:43,749 --> 00:08:48,383
Even with all that efficiency, the company's electricity demand jumped

149
00:08:48,383 --> 00:08:53,018
37 percent in 2025, the biggest increase in its history.

150
00:08:53,526 --> 00:08:56,005
That's why tech companies are hunting for

151
00:08:56,005 --> 00:08:58,130
power anywhere they can find it.

152
00:08:58,080 --> 00:09:01,983
In 2024, Microsoft signed a twenty-year deal to buy the

153
00:09:01,983 --> 00:09:05,886
output of a reactor at Three Mile Island in Pennsylvania.

154
00:09:05,886 --> 00:09:10,953
Constellation, the owner, aims to restart it around 2027.

155
00:09:11,468 --> 00:09:14,182
And no, it's not the reactor

156
00:09:14,182 --> 00:09:16,897
that partially melted down in 1979.

157
00:09:16,847 --> 00:09:19,107
That was Unit 2.

158
00:09:19,107 --> 00:09:23,416
This is Unit 1, which ran until 2019.

159
00:09:23,938 --> 00:09:27,656
Other companies are signing deals for small modular reactors,

160
00:09:27,656 --> 00:09:31,375
grid batteries, and huge amounts of wind and solar.

161
00:09:31,824 --> 00:09:35,085
None of this tells you who wins or loses.

162
00:09:35,035 --> 00:09:37,681
It's just physics and plumbing.

163
00:09:37,681 --> 00:09:40,700
More computing means more heat, and more heat

164
00:09:40,700 --> 00:09:43,719
means more power coming in through that substation.

165
00:09:44,672 --> 00:09:46,577
Quick recap.

166
00:09:46,527 --> 00:09:50,263
A single text prompt uses very little energy.

167
00:09:50,263 --> 00:09:53,162
But there are billions of them, plus training,

168
00:09:53,162 --> 00:09:56,061
plus newer and heavier kinds of AI work.

169
00:09:56,566 --> 00:09:59,806
Power comes in at high voltage, gets stepped down,

170
00:09:59,806 --> 00:10:03,045
and is backed up by batteries and diesel generators.

171
00:10:02,995 --> 00:10:06,100
An AI rack can draw around 120

172
00:10:06,100 --> 00:10:09,205
kilowatts, about fifteen times an average one.

173
00:10:09,708 --> 00:10:12,610
Nearly all of that becomes heat.

174
00:10:12,560 --> 00:10:15,591
And PUE tells you how much extra a building burns

175
00:10:15,591 --> 00:10:18,318
just to stay cool and keep the lights on.

176
00:10:18,818 --> 00:10:21,241
Now I want to hear from you.

177
00:10:21,191 --> 00:10:24,808
Is there a data center going up near where you live?

178
00:10:24,808 --> 00:10:27,601
Tell me your state in the comments.

179
00:10:27,601 --> 00:10:29,795
I'm curious how many of you have

180
00:10:29,795 --> 00:10:31,989
one being built right down the road.

181
00:10:32,512 --> 00:10:35,122
Next time, we'll follow this power back upstream

182
00:10:35,122 --> 00:10:37,732
to the grid itself, and why hooking up

183
00:10:37,732 --> 00:10:40,015
a new data center can take years.

184
00:10:39,965 --> 00:10:43,077
If you like seeing how the hidden machinery of

185
00:10:43,077 --> 00:10:46,189
modern life really works, subscribe to The Hidden Grid.
